        {
          "position": 4,
          "result": "9-11",
          "cells": [
            "9-11",
            "More friendly and benign than you’d expect them to be, given the circumstances"
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 5,
          "result": "12+",
          "cells": [
            "12+",
            "As friendly and helpful as their nature and the situation permits them to be"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "rulesText": "Suppose a GM’s just rolled a 12 for the PCs’ roadside encounter with a band of zealous blood cultists. The GM knows that the cultists are vicious but have no pressing reason to immediately attack the PCs, so what does “friendly” mean in such a situation? If you find yourself having to figure out plausible reactions for otherwise-hostile groups, here are some suggestions.\n\nThey demand a bribe. They’ll take money, gear, food, booze, praise to their dark god, or some other currency for peace.\n\nThey back off. If they aren’t defending their lair or carrying out some critical task, they may just decide to back away and keep their distance as the groups pass by. They may bring word of the adventurers to their comrades, but a fight under the present terms might look like a bad deal to them.\n\n|2d6|NPC Reaction|\n|---|---|\n|2-|As aggressively hostile as the situation allows|\n|3-5|More unfriendly and hostile than they’d be expected<br>to be in the given situation|\n|6-8|As predictably hostile or friendly as they’d usually be<br>in this situation|\n|9-11|More friendly and benign than you’d expect them to<br>be, given the circumstances|\n|12+|As friendly and helpful as their nature and the situation<br>permits them to be|\n\nThey ask for favors. Maybe they have an enemy they want killed, or a task they need done. They may trade offers of ignoring the PCs while they remain in the area in exchange for the help, or offer an outright bribe to the PCs to get them to cooperate. Even the most hostile raider group might be willing to let a few targets go in exchange for some profitable work being done.\n\nThey offer tribute. They’ve looked at the PCs and decided that their own lives are in danger. They’ll offer wealth, information, services, or other inducements to get the PCs to leave them alone. This reaction grows more possible if the PCs have been carving a bloody swath through their surroundings.\n\nThey willingly socialize. Maybe the bandits have been out here so long that they’re lonely for civilized conversation, and the PCs look too dangerous to engage. The goblin raiders might’ve mistaken them for allied marauders and invite them to share their camp. The vile necromancer might consider herself a perfectly respectable person who loves good dinner conversation. However it’s sliced, the NPC could be willing just to have a nice chat."
    },
